      creative_originality: [74, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_delacroix_19thc, src_delacroix_csm, src_delacroix_baudelaire | 1820s-1860 | scholar + press + friend] The scholarly review says colour was his defining strength and guided viewers to a painting's emotional content; the Monitor says his journal records small visual details such as a stirrup's shadow on a horse's flank; Baudelaire describes a palette composed with minute care. Attention to colour and visual detail is documented, but the strongest statements are reviewers' judgements of the outcome. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 80 / 0.62 because the high score leaned on retrospective praise of the paintings rather than on documented behaviour, and the behavioural evidence (journal detail, palette care) is brief.
      aesthetic_sensitivity: [74, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_delacroix_19thc, src_delacroix_musee, src_delacroix_baudelaire, src_delacroix_csm | 1820s-1860 | scholar + museum + friend] He worked across large easel pictures, mural cycles in four Paris settings, lithographs and watercolours (scholarly review, museum), wrote essays on Poussin, Prud'hon and Charlet for periodicals (Baudelaire), and kept a journal from the age of 24 (Monitor). The range is real but mostly within painting and its neighbouring graphic and written forms, hence a moderate score; Baudelaire says he found the pen hard.
      cross_domain_range: [60, 0.5, "s", "N"],
