    sources: [{ id: "src_servetus_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Michael Servetus (Q185914), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-04): label 'Michael Servetus', description 'Spanish physician and theologian', birth recorded as 1511 with day variants 19 and 29 September, death 1553-10-27, Korean label 미카엘 세르베투스. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q185914" }, { id: "src_servetus_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Michael Servetus' (Britannica Editors), article body read in full. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: Spanish physician and theologian (born 1511?, died 27 October 1553) whose unorthodox teachings led to condemnation as a heretic by Protestants and Roman Catholics and to execution by Calvinists from Geneva; De Trinitatis erroribus (1531), Dialogorum de Trinitate (1532), the annotated Ptolemy Geographia and Pagnini Bible editions at Lyon, medical studies at Paris from 1538, physician to the archbishop of Vienne, Christianismi restitutio (1553, 1,000 copies printed secretly at Vienne) in which he 'almost incidentally' made known his discovery of the pulmonary circulation of blood; his execution produced a Protestant controversy on the death penalty for heresy and influenced Laelius Socinus. Scholarly reference; modern summary.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Michael-Servetus" }, { id: "src_servetus_eb1911", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, eleventh edition (1911), 'Servetus, Michael', as transcribed on Wikisource, the passages on Vienne, the printing of the book, the 1553 arrest and trial, and his doctrine read: twelve years (1541-53) as confidential physician at Vienne while outwardly a conforming Catholic; the 1546 correspondence with Calvin and Calvin's remark that he would not let him leave Geneva alive; two Basel publishers declined the book; secret printing of 1,000 copies; interrogation and escape from Vienne prison; arrest at Geneva after attending church; a sentence on 26 October and execution at Champel; the only likeness is a small 1607 copperplate by C. van Sichem whose original is not known and whose authenticity is uncertain; his system 'not easily described in terms of current systems' and abhorrent to Catholics and Protestants alike. Older scholarly reference, independent of the modern Britannica article; its judgments are dated.", url: "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Servetus,_Michael" }, { id: "src_servetus_uudb", kind: "biography", title: "Dictionary of Unitarian and Universalist Biography (Unitarian Universalist Historical Society), 'Servetus, Michael', full entry read: dated life from Villanueva in Aragon and law at Toulouse, service under Juan de Quintana, Basel and Strasbourg, the 1531 book and the 1532 Dialogues that did not soften his rhetoric, flight to Paris as Villeneuve after the Spanish Inquisition summoned him, medical studies and dissection under Gunther in 1536-38, the 1538 dispute with the Paris faculty over astrology, editorial work for Lyon printers, twelve years at Vienne, the letters to Calvin and Calvin's reaction, arrest and Geneva trial, Castellio's reply; the author notes that because Servetus expressed the new circulation knowledge only as a metaphor in a proscribed theological work it did not influence Harvey, and that 'most persons lack sufficient understanding of his views'. Unitarian historical society entry: sympathetic to him but a scholarly, dated narrative.", url: "https://www.uudb.org/?p=6398" }, { id: "src_servetus_schaff", kind: "archive", title: "Philip Schaff, History of the Christian Church, Volume VIII (1892), section on the trial of Servetus (Christian Classics Ethereal Library), full section read: how the Vienne authorities learned of the book through Guillaume Trie, the Vienne interrogations in which Schaff records that Servetus gave false answers about his identity and writings and said he had once personated 'Servetus' in correspondence, his escape on 7 April 1553, the Vienne sentence of 17 June, burning in effigy, and Schaff's footnote that Servetus himself printed thirty of his letters to Calvin in the book. A Reformed Protestant church historian writing in 1892: a critical, orthodox-leaning counter-voice that treats Calvin's role as indirect agency and an excess of zeal; its moral judgments are not used.", url: "https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/hcc8.iv.xvi.xiii.html" }, { id: "src_servetus_ebsco", kind: "institution", title: "EBSCO Research Starters, 'Michael Servetus Describes the Circulatory System', full entry read: law and mathematics studies, editing medical works for Lyon publishers, Paris medical studies with anatomical dissection and praise from the anatomy professor Johann Gunther von Andernach, his questioning of the Galenic claim of pores in the heart septum, the description of blood passing from the right ventricle through the lungs to the left, its theological setting in the discussion of the Holy Spirit and 'triple spirit', and the failed search for a willing publisher before secretly printing the work at his own expense. A modern encyclopaedic overview aimed at students; a few of its statements (dating the discovery to 1546, 'published posthumously') conflict with the other sources and are not used.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/michael-servetus-describes-circulatory-system" }, { id: "src_servetus_ct", kind: "press", title: "Christianity Today, Christian History, 'The Servetus Affair' (1986), full article read: presents the trial from a Calvinist-sympathetic angle, says Servetus had been condemned in absentia in Catholic and Protestant Europe, that he went to Geneva after escaping a Catholic prison, was recognised while Calvin was preaching, that Calvin visited him in jail and, when sentence was passed, asked the city government for a more humane death. A short popular article from an evangelical magazine; used as the Calvin-side counter-voice, not as evidence of Servetus's motives.", url: "https://www.christianitytoday.com/1986/10/servetus-affair/" }, { id: "src_servetus_bosmia", kind: "institution", title: "University of Texas Medical Branch research record for A. Bosmia and colleagues, 'Michael Servetus (1511-1553): Physician and heretic who described the pulmonary circulation', International Journal of Cardiology 167 (2013), pages 318-321: ABSTRACT ONLY was read (the article is paywalled): a peer-reviewed review that says writers have praised Servetus's commitment to educating colleagues about what he heralded as the truth and criticised his perceived arrogance, and that he contributed to geography, astrology, theology and medicine. Used only to confirm the medical-history framing; not used for any row.", url: "https://researchexperts.utmb.edu/en/publications/michael-servetus-1511-1553-physician-and-heretic-who-described-th/" }, { id: "src_servetus_parismusees", kind: "archive", title: "Paris Musees collections, Musee Carnavalet - Histoire de Paris, 'Michael Servetus Hisp. de Aragonia', engraving by Christian-Friedrich Fritzsch, 18th century, inventory G.19347 (item page, marked CC0). Holder record for the portrait only.", url: "https://www.parismuseescollections.paris.fr/fr/musee-carnavalet/oeuvres/michael-servetus-hs-de-arajonia" }],
