      // [src_servetus_britannica, src_servetus_eb1911, src_servetus_uudb, src_servetus_ebsco | 1531-1553 | encyclopaedia, older encyclopaedia, Unitarian biography, overview] Britannica says that in De Trinitatis erroribus (1531) he attacked the orthodox teaching on the Trinity and tried to form a view of his own, and that the book was clearly odious to Catholics and Protestants; the 1911 Britannica calls his system marked by strong individuality and hard to describe in terms of current systems; the Unitarian dictionary records that his 1532 Dialogues conceded nothing important and did not soften his rhetoric, and EBSCO that he questioned the Galenic pores in the heart septum. Several independent sources agree on theological independence; the medical independence rests on two sources, and the theology was heterodox in ways that cost him dearly (dual-edged), so confidence stays below 0.6.
