      // [src_servetus_britannica, src_servetus_uudb, src_servetus_eb1911, src_servetus_ebsco | 1530s-1553 | encyclopaedia, Unitarian biography, older encyclopaedia, overview] Documented work in law, geography (editions of Ptolemy's Geographia in 1535 and 1541 and geography lectures), a Bible edition with introduction and notes, astrology and medicine (Paris studies 1536-38 with dissection, then twelve years as a physician), and theology; the 1911 Britannica says he constantly displayed a passion for original discovery in all departments and Bosmia's abstract lists geography, astrology, theology and medicine. Breadth is well documented, but editing for Lyon printers was paid work, and depth in each field is uneven (the astrology work drew censure), hence moderate confidence.
      cross_domain_range: [76, 0.55, "s", "A"],
      // [src_servetus_uudb, src_servetus_eb1911, src_servetus_ebsco, src_servetus_britannica | 1527-1553 | Unitarian biography, older encyclopaedia, overview, encyclopaedia] The Unitarian dictionary says the 1531 book took four years of work and that he followed it with a revised formulation; Britannica and the 1911 Britannica record that he kept revising the manuscript of Christianismi restitutio from 1546, that two Basel publishers declined it, and that he had it secretly printed in 1,000 copies in 1553 while living as a physician at Vienne; EBSCO says he failed to find a willing publisher and printed at his own expense. A long, repeated effort on one project, but the timeline of the revisions rests on secondary summaries and persistence of this kind also carried risk.
      persistence: [72, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_servetus_uudb, src_servetus_britannica, src_servetus_schaff, src_servetus_eb1911 | 1530-1553 | Unitarian biography, encyclopaedia, church history, older encyclopaedia] The Unitarian dictionary says he wore out his welcome in Oecolampadius's household with constant theological dispute, then bombarded Calvin with letters insisting on more radical views, returned an annotated copy of Calvin's Institutes with abusive annotations, and (with Schaff and Britannica) printed thirty of his letters to Calvin in the book. Against that, Schaff and the 1911 Britannica record that before the Vienne inquisitors he gave false answers about his identity and writings and said he had personated 'Servetus' in correspondence, and he escaped prison; he engaged in open dispute with the Reformers but avoided it with the authorities who could punish him. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): documented denial and evasion before the Vienne inquisitors cuts against the original 70/0.50 reading, so the row was lowered to 64/0.45.
      conflict_tolerance: [64, 0.45, "s", "D"],
      // [src_servetus_britannica, src_servetus_uudb, src_servetus_eb1911, src_servetus_ct | 1531-1553 | encyclopaedia, Unitarian biography, older encyclopaedia, popular article] He published an attack on Trinitarian doctrine under his own name in 1531, opened a correspondence with Calvin in 1546 and sent him the manuscript (the 1911 Britannica quotes Calvin's reply that he would not let him leave Geneva alive), had the book secretly printed at Vienne, and, after escaping the Vienne prison, was in Geneva in August 1553 (Britannica calls his appearance there 'quixotic'; the 1911 Britannica says he was on his way to Zurich and Naples and attended church to avoid trouble; his motive is not documented and is not inferred). Counter-evidence: he lived under an assumed name for roughly two decades and printed secretly, which is risk management, so the row is a mixed, low-confidence reading.
      risk_tolerance: [62, 0.42, "i", "D"],
