      // [src_xu_mingshi, src_xu_mactutor, src_xu_bdcc | 1600-1630 | official history, history-of-mathematics biography, Catholic reference] The Ming shi says he learned Ricci's astronomy, calendrical mathematics and firearms to the full extent of his teacher's skill, BDCC says he went on learning European science from de Ursis, and MacTutor says he invented the Chinese terms for point, curve, parallel line and angle types that entered Chinese mathematics. The 'to the full' is a retrospective official judgement, and MacTutor notes he had himself never read the Nine Chapters, so the row stays a low-confidence lean.
      mastery_orientation: [64, 0.48, "i", "A"],
