      impact_motivation: [58, 0.42, "i", "A"],
      // [src_xu_mingshi, src_xu_jahh, src_xu_mactutor | 1628-1629 | official history, astronomers' paper, history-of-mathematics biography] The Ming shi says that when the emperor wished to punish the astronomers for a failed eclipse forecast, Xu answered that Guo Shoujing's method had also once failed and that calendars drift and should be corrected in time, and the emperor then ordered the Jesuits to compute with Xu as supervisor; Serego Alighieri and Corsi say it appears Xu succeeded in convincing the emperor, probably also using the correct 1610 forecast. The same paper cites a study finding the Western method worse in the 1629 contest, so the success of the argument is not evidence that the method was better. Reviewed in the self-calibration pass and kept because it is already low-confidence.
      persuasiveness: [58, 0.42, "i", "A"],
